# The compiler's content hash, in Term.
#
# cyrb53: a fast, well-distributed 53-bit string hash. A collision only ever causes a stale REUSE and never a
# crash, and at 53 bits that is astronomically unlikely for a source tree.
#
# THIS IS THE CACHE KEY. Every cached mill and every cached compile is addressed by it, so it is the one function
# in the compiler where a one-bit disagreement between the TypeScript and the Term version would be invisible in
# testing and catastrophic in use: two compilers that hash differently do not share a cache, and two that hash the
# SAME WAY BUT WRONGLY serve each other stale answers. `pnpm term:self-host` compares them over every name in the
# corpus for exactly that reason.
#
# IT COULD NOT BE WRITTEN IN TERM UNTIL 2026-09-02. cyrb53 is defined in terms of `Math.imul`, a signed 32-bit
# multiply that DISCARDS the high bits, and the stdlib had only ordinary multiplication. A 64-bit product gives a
# different number, so the hash would not have been cyrb53. `bit/multiply-32` was added for this and is the first
# language gap the self-hosting port closed.

load @term/base/bit
  find bitwise-exclusive-or
  find unsigned-shift-right
  find multiply-32
  find bitwise-and

load @term/base/text
  find split
  find char-code-at

# the two seeds cyrb53 starts from
host seed-one, code 3735928559

host seed-two, code 1103547991

# ONE PASS OVER THE TEXT. This walked an index and asked `char-code-at(text, at)` for each one, and on node a
# code-point-aware `char-code-at` scans from the start of the string, so hashing was O(n^2) in the file's length:
# 1.9 of 2.4 seconds compiling a 40-deep nest of forks went here, and the stdlib's 24,826-line Unicode table would
# have taken minutes per cache key. Walking the text visits each character once, and the code of a one-character
# text is constant time on every backend. Found 2026-10-02, the day this module became the compiler's only hash.
task hash-text
  take text, like text

  like text

  save one, seed-one
  save two, seed-two

  # the characters, split once: `split` with an empty separator splits by code point on every backend (Array.from
  # on node), which is one pass, where a walk over indexes paid a scan per index
  save chars
    split read(text), <>
  walk chars
    take site, name char
    save code, char-code-at char, code 0
    save one
      multiply-32 bitwise-exclusive-or(one, read code), code 2654435761
    save two
      multiply-32 bitwise-exclusive-or(two, read code), code 1597334677
  save one-mixed
    bitwise-exclusive-or
      multiply-32 bitwise-exclusive-or(one, shift-32 one, code 16), code 2246822507
      multiply-32 bitwise-exclusive-or(two, shift-32 two, code 13), code 3266489909
  save two-mixed
    bitwise-exclusive-or
      multiply-32 bitwise-exclusive-or(two, shift-32 two, code 16), code 2246822507
      multiply-32
        bitwise-exclusive-or one-mixed, shift-32 one-mixed, code 13
        code 3266489909
  # `4294967296 * (2097151 & h2) + (h1 >>> 0)`, then base 36
  save high, bitwise-and two-mixed, code 2097151
  save low, shift-32 one-mixed, code 0

  back
    to-base-36
      add multiply(code(4294967296), high), low

# `>>>` IS A 32-BIT SHIFT IN JAVASCRIPT and the stdlib's `unsigned-shift-right` is 64-bit, so a negative
# intermediate shifts in a completely different number. Masking to the low 32 bits first is what makes the two
# agree. This is the second thing cyrb53 needed that Term did not have a direct word for.
task shift-32
  take value, like number
  take count, like number

  like number

  back
    unsigned-shift-right bitwise-and(value, code 4294967295), count

# a non-negative integer in base 36, which is how the key is spelled on disk
task to-base-36
  take value, like number

  like text

  back call value/toString, code 36
